when it comes to iconic hats  nothing says French more than the beret  specifically the Basque style beret as  people wear different styles in other  countries the Basque beret was  traditionally worn by Shepherds tending  their flocks along the Pyrenees mountain  range that divides southern France from  Spain  the vast beret dates back to the 1600s  in southern France made of black merino  wool it has a little cabby you on top  the factory making these braised has  been doing so since 1840 an automated  machine knits undyed wool yarn into a  galette the French word for pancake the  galette is flat and round with loose  knit stitches making it soft and floppy  a worker then pulls the galette central  thread closing it into a circular shape  resembling a shower cap in an  industrial-sized washing machine a  worker washes this rudimentary beret and  lukewarm water from the local river and  soap for about eight hours the type of  soap is a trade secret  the wolf shrinks causing the loose knit  stitches to tighten the wool fibers also  softened transforming into the smooth  and soft woolen fabric we call felt  after felting the beret the  manufacturers begin the dyeing process  the worker loads it into another washing  machine only this time with powder dye  this Factory has always produced its own  dyes in a wide range of colors again  using water from the local river however  the temperature to which this company  heats it is a closely guarded secret  [Music]  when the dye cycle finishes another  worker checks the beret against a color  sample because the wool composition and  the river water quality are variable  factors that can affect dye absorption  [Music]  the next step is to form the beret to  the final shape  traditionally beret factories did this  using wooden forums but today they use  metal ones the beret remains on the form  long enough to lock in the shape this  process too depends on the wool  composition as well as on how humid it  is in the factory on that particular day  each beret goes for a spin on the  scratching machine a rotating wheel  surfaced with wire brushes the bristles  lift the wood fibers thickening the felt  [Music]  after inserting a cardboard template to  maintain the shape a worker uses a  manual shearing tool to carefully  scratch the area around the cab EU she  removes any clinging plant debris left  by the river water used in the wash and  die cycles then she inspects for any  holes or stretched fibres  the beret now goes to the seamstress  first she heats a black cotton lining on  a mold to shape it to fit perfectly  inside the beret the lining has the  manufacturers label in the center which  features the company crest and the model  name true Basque confirming that this is  indeed an authentic Basque style beret  she turns the Brahe inside out to sew in  the lining  [Music]  then she turns the beret right-side out  again and puts it on a stretching  machine she sets the machine to the  desired hat size and it automatically  stretches the beret to that size  [Music]  now she sews a black leather band to the  edge the Hat size is printed on it some  models feature a color trim on the band  the finishing touch is the company's  metal hallmark she rivets it to the  underside of the beret on one side  [Music]  and that caps off production  [Music]  you
 

